msgctxt ""
"\n    Anything a model wrote is marked as such where it lands: a value "
"written by\n    the assistant is recorded with the model that wrote it "
"and the person who\n    asked, and the record shows it. A person can "
"accept it, edit it or throw it\n    away, and what they did is recorded "
"too. Nothing is written to a record\n    without somebody asking for "
"it.\n"
msgid "ai"
msgstr "KI"

msgctxt ""
"\n    Language models get things wrong confidently. The assistant is a "
"drafting\n    tool and nothing it produces is advice — legal, financial, "
"medical or\n    otherwise. Check what it writes before you rely on it, "
"and do not use it to\n    make a decision about a person that has a "
"legal or similarly significant\n    effect on them.\n"
msgid "ai"
msgstr "KI"

msgctxt ""
"\n    Nothing you send to a model is used to train it. We do not train "
"models\n    ourselves, and we use the providers' APIs on terms that "
"exclude training on\n    customer input. Prompts and completions are "
"retained only as long as needed\n    to produce the answer and to keep "
"the usage record that bills it.\n"
msgid "ai"
msgstr "KI"

msgctxt ""
"\n    OneSpace includes an assistant and a number of features that use "
"large\n    language models: writing a field's value, summarising a "
"record, answering a\n    question about a screen. They are optional; a "
"workspace can turn every one\n    of them off, and they do nothing until "
"somebody asks.\n"
msgid "ai"
msgstr "KI"

